nswer/id #uuid "e2824d5c-06ca-59b3-b7b1-7ae1f409e8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117079} {:answer/id #uuid "172c1bf2-413c-5f22-88a6-2dc218007d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117080} {:answer/id #uuid "4616856c-2742-517b-b0bd-4393a99aa584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117081}] :answer-field/id #uuid "51d57ab6-5489-5cb2-b79e-e32e21c7ffcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a240832-7ec5-5893-8c91-6e7fe3f7f4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376036} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6abdfb-7399-521b-b7a9-dcdf167198fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376037} {:answer/id #uuid "22ed2a79-2156-58e4-86f5-71af571c7faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376038} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf4de98-5043-503b-a333-c7425d3cc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376039} {:answer/id #uuid "807a91ad-cad7-53ce-962a-078fe3057e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf4de98-5043-503b-a333-c7425d3cc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376039} :answer-field/id #uuid "70866091-6932-5a02-81c7-ead3c6b5964c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44d1d34b-4b40-5331-8b03-073462e9a684" :question/math-academy-id "q-155956" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $x=2y$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y \\\\ (0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2b \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2a+2b \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2a \\\\ a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2ka \\\\ ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af3f4c35-be23-5a1a-97fb-18e89ab51591" :knowledge-point/title "Writing Polynomials in Standard Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049538 :topic/math-academy-id 1783 :topic/title "Simplifying Polynomials"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b58aa12-5d7a-5181-8030-339d694e215b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117200} {:answer/id #uuid "b53a74e5-b3c7-5c23-83a1-1191705b96d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117201} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d09c37-8c1f-5c30-acb5-394bd0914edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117202} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8e4341-7cec-58df-8a8f-0757f0e1114a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117203} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4cef90-0c41-5148-a899-4fd1bf8e3ff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117204}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c2df5185-1638-547d-b1c7-af1b386ac03d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117199}] :question/id #uuid "39be80b7-c677-5614-a865-529ef5a0ce5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-30326" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are given in standard form?\n\n1. $2x^{3} - 3x^{2} - 6$\n2. $6 + 4x - x^{2}$\n3. $-x^{2} + 2x^{3} + 3$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/math-academy-id 1779 :topic/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b0012c2-9e36-5ddc-b432-6223b1ea13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117290} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f8da87-77c9-5d32-8a09-1e3610169e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117291} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1b30fa-5a4e-5767-96e1-e58753e03a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117292} {:answer/id #uuid "038ab4ad-a6fd-517d-9fa5-2542608f8143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117293} {:answer/id #uuid "727efee5-a824-527e-82ff-d09ae7bd1bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb1b30fa-5a4e-5767-96e1-e58753e03a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117292} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a12d13e-2577-5a86-b53a-e88da174b659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85de5a76-468b-557f-8cba-f675615513a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-32551" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form. Although we have a product of an upper triangular matrix on the left and a unit lower triangular matrix on the right, the order of multiplication is not correct (instead, we need a unit lower triangular matrix on the left and an upper triangular matrix on the right).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048743 :topic/math-academy-id 1779 :topic/title "LU Factorization of 2x2 Matrices"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8308f719-9c9e-531e-a4ec-97b68e8d606a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117344} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec51409-e984-55df-bf9a-2a9287757947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117345} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cf7197-0f7d-5fc8-aada-e25be5df7772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117346} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5ad70c-88b9-59a4-bfa6-56e20fb74875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117347} {:answer/id #uuid "178010b4-23ca-597f-821e-15b0495bd5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117348}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2a1940e4-e4ff-5b03-a880-1641fc437faa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117343}] :question/id #uuid "9e468a23-8ad5-5652-8aab-04e323c7e93b" :question/math-academy-id "q-32607" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A$?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822ddbd5-58de-5fea-9cf7-1fcbe86f67cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117940} {:answer/id #uuid "96af24ee-f190-5ae5-95e4-2c700e3c3503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117941} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c6879f-2d58-53e5-896a-a25733cccd9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117942} {:answer/id #uuid "1c219b15-3bf1-5781-9424-d826c1ed20af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117943} {:answer/id #uuid "34cec551-a885-5fdc-95a2-f60a69416744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117944}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f729d47-2792-5b7b-b557-b37c1a7f41ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4162d50c-81cd-5f40-945e-55e7fbb90d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364079} {:answer/id #uuid "5c442bb0-6c4a-5605-97d8-7c10a412af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364080} {:answer/id #uuid "46cc0c5f-07f0-54ed-a508-6823ad071f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364081} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb77505-a33c-51be-8117-b6192c0d360c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364082} {:answer/id #uuid "641b4be1-7f54-5a77-b6cb-f23af77a0838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "641b4be1-7f54-5a77-b6cb-f23af77a0838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364083} :answer-field/id #uuid "c92c8641-7770-5cbc-adff-e1f92e5b0f87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd1b519a-6daa-58c6-a554-f53dcb225d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34153" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-fiel