= 3$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5277711-d71e-5c9c-bc97-a6c710c48803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121729} {:answer/id #uuid "eba411a2-c20e-5ebf-b1b8-adff8205bc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121730} {:answer/id #uuid "21b083f8-a00e-5fa5-a259-4259ac546288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121731} {:answer/id #uuid "3623d2d6-bfdf-580d-88b9-6901a9b94c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121732} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9a283f-e942-5c67-9a3b-3ba6fd147e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121733}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c6fceefb-5995-5f76-9799-63e4dea66b95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121728} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140932fa-f586-563d-9607-96c917cbdb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379599} {:answer/id #uuid "2c28fd72-ece1-5c65-9fc9-d396faabcac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379600} {:answer/id #uuid "6a65f6c1-17f8-5e2d-a643-c8eb1959ae64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379601} {:answer/id #uuid "d801c7c1-24b0-593b-bc5b-d8e84efc07fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379602} {:answer/id #uuid "82b95097-cf5b-58e9-bb1d-b10c2e812f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c28fd72-ece1-5c65-9fc9-d396faabcac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379600} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d17c4ab-d6be-5b30-b706-494795e0cd33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fc9b8ec-a9a8-566c-b5b3-f80f696a5223" :question/math-academy-id "q-341230" :question/problem "$$\n(x-1){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=1.$\n- The functions $(x-1)P(x)$ and $(x-1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=1.$\n- The point $x=1$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=1,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=1.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\frac{1}{x-1}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=1$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=1.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=1$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=1$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81eb3d4e-02e6-5388-b26d-fbb95910c19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121735} {:answer/id #uuid "8954d457-57f8-579e-a866-f07e4462dc92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121736} {:answer/id #uuid "b42a1452-7d84-5b29-a70a-fa33a98de323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121737} {:answer/id #uuid "00617ae7-73b5-560e-a064-6456bf95c762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121